WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.100
Speaker 1: Welcome back, everybody. Good to be&nbsp;
back. And so much to discuss. We'll start with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.100 --> 00:00:12.840
the situation in Russia, a failed coup attempt&nbsp;
which Vladimir Putin has survived. But to what&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:12.840 --> 00:00:18.300
end and with what long term impact, we just don't&nbsp;
know. But let's go through what took place over&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:18.300 --> 00:00:24.420
the weekend. Incredible. The Wagner Group. Wagner&nbsp;
group, everybody has their own way of pronouncing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:24.420 --> 00:00:32.880
it, launched a coup attempt by sending tanks and&nbsp;
troops towards the Russian capital of Moscow,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:32.880 --> 00:00:39.360
hoping to overthrow Vladimir Putin and put&nbsp;
in some kind of puppet leader. Quite frankly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:39.360 --> 00:00:48.540
the mercenary group, the Wagner group, is led by&nbsp;
Yevgeny Prigozhin, formerly a close associate of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:48.540 --> 00:00:54.300
Vladimir Putin, sanctioned by the U.S., for&nbsp;
his role in interfering in the 2016 and 2020&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:54.300 --> 00:01:04.260
elections. And Prigozhin alliance with Putin&nbsp;
soured when he accused Putin. Rather, he accused&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:04.260 --> 00:01:11.100
the Russian defense minister of incompetence and&nbsp;
betrayal, claiming that he had ordered attacks&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:11.100 --> 00:01:17.520
on Wagner's troops in Ukraine and elsewhere. The&nbsp;
timeline, as far as we understand it, is that on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:17.520 --> 00:01:25.200
Friday, June 23rd, Prigozhin threatened rebellion&nbsp;
Saturday, June 24th, very early, just hours later,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:25.200 --> 00:01:30.660
one in the morning on Saturday, Vladimir Putin&nbsp;
was briefed about the armed rebellion that was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:30.660 --> 00:01:38.220
being threatened. By 7:30 a.m., the Wagner group&nbsp;
claimed control over Rostov military facilities&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:38.220 --> 00:01:46.500
and the airport. 2 hours later, the march towards&nbsp;
Moscow began. 10 a.m., Vladimir Putin addressed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:46.500 --> 00:01:55.080
Russia on television, calling the acts treasonous.&nbsp;
And by that night, 8 p.m. prigozhin as far as we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:55.080 --> 00:02:02.820
currently understood understand halted the march&nbsp;
on Moscow. The reports are that the coup attempt&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:02.820 --> 00:02:10.020
was foiled by the bell of Russian President&nbsp;
Lukashenko brokering a deal between Putin&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:10.020 --> 00:02:18.180
and Prigozhin, allowing Prigozhin to escape to&nbsp;
Belarus in exchange for some kind of safe haven.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:18.180 --> 00:02:24.900
Presumably Prigozhin has failed. Coup attempt also&nbsp;
has shown shifting public opinion about him and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:24.900 --> 00:02:30.600
the Wagner group and quite frankly, the ethical&nbsp;
dilemmas of supporting or opposing coups based on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:30.600 --> 00:02:37.380
who it is that is doing them. This is a critical&nbsp;
aspect to the story. Prigozhin coup attempt&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:37.980 --> 00:02:44.220
had mixed reactions among both Russians and the&nbsp;
international community when news broke of this.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:44.220 --> 00:02:50.040
And in fact, I have some Ukrainian friends who,&nbsp;
with tears in their eyes, wrote out, wrote to me,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:50.040 --> 00:02:53.880
and I could tell even though they were texting,&nbsp;
that they had tears in their eyes. They were happy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:53.880 --> 00:02:58.860
about this. They welcomed it as a challenge to&nbsp;
Vladimir Putin's, of course, authoritarian rule&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:00.060 --> 00:03:06.060
and the idea that maybe there could be&nbsp;
democratic change in Russia. Others condemned&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:06.060 --> 00:03:10.500
the coup attempt, saying it's reckless, it's a&nbsp;
violent act, it threatens Russia's stability, it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:10.500 --> 00:03:16.560
threatens Tibetans, Russian security. Even if the&nbsp;
coup were to succeed, it's it does not set Russia&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:16.560 --> 00:03:22.620
and the Russian people up for really dramatically&nbsp;
improved circumstances. There are those who say a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:22.620 --> 00:03:28.560
coup is never justified, regardless of the regime&nbsp;
that they are targeting, because coups undermine&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:28.560 --> 00:03:33.240
the rule of law, they undermine the will of the&nbsp;
people. Others say, well, sometimes the rule of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:33.240 --> 00:03:38.220
law and the will of the people has been undermined&nbsp;
by the strongman who is in power already. And the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:38.220 --> 00:03:43.200
coup might be the only way to actually get back&nbsp;
on track. These are the various opinions that have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:43.200 --> 00:03:52.440
come forward. One position is that it's okay to&nbsp;
support coups if they are against undemocratically&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:52.440 --> 00:03:59.280
elected leaders. Can it be said that Putin was&nbsp;
elected democratically? Well, he says he was.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:59.280 --> 00:04:07.380
There is this ostensible democracy, but was it?&nbsp;
Putin won his fourth presidential term with 77% of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:07.380 --> 00:04:15.600
the vote in an election that was widely criticized&nbsp;
by domestic and international observers for,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:15.600 --> 00:04:23.460
to put it lightly, irregularities and violations.&nbsp;
So is Putin actually democratically elected?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:23.460 --> 00:04:30.600
Putin's main opponents were either barred&nbsp;
from running or jailed, or even worse,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:30.600 --> 00:04:38.100
Putin's critics were excluded from the vote on&nbsp;
constitutional reforms, which allowed Putin to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:38.100 --> 00:04:46.080
reset term limits and run for two more six year&nbsp;
terms. That's not democracy. But does that mean we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:46.080 --> 00:04:51.900
support a coup attempt? And then, of course, the&nbsp;
the other part of this is that Prigozhin himself&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:52.620 --> 00:05:01.020
initially seen by some favorably as someone&nbsp;
doing a good. Being worthy of the international&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:01.020 --> 00:05:08.940
left's support. And that very quickly came apart&nbsp;
once more was learned about Prigozhin and Wagner&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:08.940 --> 00:05:15.720
groups activities in different theaters around the&nbsp;
world. So that is where things stand right now,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:15.720 --> 00:05:23.100
as far as we know. There is not total transparency&nbsp;
about news regarding exactly what is going on,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:23.100 --> 00:05:28.560
but the broader and more forward looking question&nbsp;
to which I just don't think we have an answer. If&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:28.560 --> 00:05:35.064
you Google what comes next in Russia, what happens&nbsp;
next to Putin? You will find several dozen op&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:35.064 --> 00:05:42.420
eds from the last 24 to 48 hours know to agree&nbsp;
about what is coming next. And in particular,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:42.420 --> 00:05:51.780
for Vladimir Putin, the most optimistic sort of&nbsp;
blended scenario that I've seen proposals put&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:51.780 --> 00:06:00.360
forth. Is that now with the threat of the&nbsp;
coup, but the coup having failed. Vladimir&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:00.360 --> 00:06:08.640
Putin may realize his days are numbered and&nbsp;
will engineer some kind of face saving exit&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:08.640 --> 00:06:15.360
from power to avoid being deposed or whatever.&nbsp;
I just don't know that that's really the most&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:15.360 --> 00:06:20.580
likely scenario here. So that's the latest we&nbsp;
have out of Russia as of this moment. We're&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:20.580 --> 00:06:24.420
going to continue following it. We'll probably&nbsp;
have more for you tomorrow or the next day

